        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: stop pinning stale cost snapshots via scanner debounce",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nFixes #2089. App-managed token-cost refreshes could accept a scanner result still inside the scanner's 60-second cache debounce, then pin that stale result for the app's one-hour token-cost TTL.\n\nThe repair adds an app-only `bypassScannerDebounce` seam. `UsageStore` opts into it because it already owns refresh throttling; public `CostUsageFetcher` callers such as CLI serve retain the existing scanner debounce. The bypass applies consistently to native and Pi scanners, while forced refresh behavior remains unchanged.\n\nThis keeps the scope in the app orchestration layer instead of modifying vendored scanner options or inferring caller intent from a default-value sentinel.\n\n## Regression proof\n\nThe focused fixture performs three reads against one appended Codex history file:\n\n1. Initial read observes 110 tokens.\n2. A direct non-forced read inside the debounce window remains at 110.\n3. An app-style read with the bypass observes the appended total of 176.\n\n## Commands run\n\n```bash\nswift test --filter CostUsageFetcherTests\nmake check\nmake test\n```\n\nResults: 12/12 focused tests; 633/633 full-suite selections across 53 groups; zero failures, retries, or timeouts. Independent autoreview reported no actionable findings.\n\n## Issue\n\nFixes #2089\n",
